When you arrive at an emergency animal hospital, the clock is already running and the room is loud. Here is what helps your dog the most — straight from the people on the other side of the door.

## Bring history, not panic

Snap a photo on your phone of any current medications, recent food packaging, and your regular vet's contact information before you leave the house. The first ten minutes of intake go faster when we don't have to chase paperwork.

## Tell us the timeline

What changed, and when. "He's been off for two days" tells us something different than "He collapsed twenty minutes ago." Time is information.

## You can ask about cost up front

It is okay — and we want you to — ask about the estimate before you authorize treatment. A written estimate is normal, expected, and how good emergency hospitals operate. If the number is impossible and your dog's situation is treatable, ask whether your hospital has resources, payment plans, or partner organizations like ours that can help.

## Trust your gut

You know your dog better than anyone. If something feels wrong, that's a real data point. We'd rather see a healthy dog twice than miss the one that needed us.

## We're rooting for you

The hardest part of our job is the conversation about money on the worst day of your life. The reason organizations like The Wolf Project exist is so that conversation has another path. If you ever find yourself in that room, ask. The worst we can say is "not this case." But we'd rather hear about you than not.

